# SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD
"""lib/rtl/mimic_time.pas against CPython's real `time`.

A DIFFERENTIAL: this file runs unmodified under CPython and produces identical
output both ways. Run it as `python3 test/lib_mimic_time.npy` to see that.

EVERY ROW IS A PREDICATE, NOT A CLOCK READING, for the obvious reason -- the
numbers differ on every run and between the two runtimes. So what is asserted is
the part of the contract that does not vary: ordering, origin, and resolution.

THE ORIGIN ROWS ARE THE LOAD-BEARING ONES. `monotonic()` and `time()` would both
satisfy "does not go backwards over two adjacent reads", so that row alone cannot
tell them apart and would pass if the shim wired both to the same clock -- which
is exactly the plausible mistake, since the PAL reaches both through one
PalClockGetTime call differing only in a clock id. `monotonic() < 1e9` and
`time() > 1.7e9` separate them: one is boot-relative, the other is an epoch, and
no single clock can answer both.

NO NEGATIVE-SLEEP ROW. `sleep(-1)` raises ValueError in CPython and returns here,
which is a CHOSEN divergence in the permitted direction (CPython rejects that
program) -- recorded in devdocs/dev/nilpy-semantics-divergences.md. An oracle
that raises cannot produce an expected line, so a divergence in this direction is
untestable by differential by construction. It is asserted nowhere; the note is
the record.

THE RESOLUTION ROW IS WHY THIS IS NOT GetTickCount64. That is
PalMonotonicMillis -- correctly monotonic, and milliseconds, which is 6% of a
frame at 60fps and lands in a dt that drives physics. The row below would pass on
a millisecond clock roughly 1 time in 1000, so treat a failure there as real and
a pass as weak evidence; the strong evidence is the unit under test reading
nanoseconds from PalClockGetTime directly.
"""

import time

a = time.monotonic()
b = time.monotonic()
print("monotonic does not go back   ", b >= a)
print("monotonic origin is not epoch", a < 1000000000.0)

print("time is an epoch             ", time.time() > 1700000000.0)
print("time does not go back        ", time.time() >= time.time() - 1.0)

t0 = time.monotonic()
time.sleep(0.05)
slept = time.monotonic() - t0
print("sleep(0.05) >= 0.04          ", slept >= 0.04)
print("sleep(0.05) < 1.0            ", slept < 1.0)

time.sleep(0)
print("sleep(0) returned            ", True)

print("finer than a millisecond     ", (time.monotonic() * 1000.0) % 1.0 != 0.0)
